        "Now go back to the season one figures and do the arithmetic nobody in the piece does.  <mark>9 million dollars of sales against 200 million views is about four and a half cents of sales per view.</mark>  Said out loud that sounds bad.  It only sounds bad if you have never priced 200 million paid impressions, which is the comparison the number is actually asking for.  Bob's did not get those views cheaply because the show was brilliant.  It got them because the cast arrived with the audience attached.",
        "One caution on the 50 percent.  It is the brand's own figure, measured against its own prior year, with no absolute view count attached to season two and no disclosure of what either season cost to make.  A brand reporting that its newest thing is beating its older thing is the least surprising sentence in marketing.  The structural point survives the discount, because the variable they changed is specific and stated.",
